The embedding model from a folder of the image: EMBEDDING_MODEL_PATH

What: TransformResources passes EMBEDDING_MODEL_PATH to fastembed as specific_model_path, so the batches
load the model from that folder instead of fastembed's download; fastembed keeps its own description of
EMBEDDING_MODEL (pooling, normalisation, tokenizer files from the folder). The folder's marker
modama-model.json must name EMBEDDING_MODEL, or the batch stops before embedding. The log line names the
folder. Unset or empty, nothing changes.

Why: the modama search stack's image puts there the same graph stored in float32 (embedding-model/fp32.py).
The file fastembed downloads (qdrant/bge-small-en-v1.5-onnx-q, model_optimized.onnx) is a float16 graph;
ONNX Runtime's CPU provider has no float16 kernels for its fused nodes and casts the activations around
them on every run. In the stack's VM (one process, 1 thread, sub-batch 32, sorted) the float32 file took
14 % less CPU per text on short texts, 10 % on mixed and 13 % on long (512 tokens), with a minimum cosine
of 0.9999997 to the downloaded file's vectors, not bitwise identical (change embedding-fp32-model, E5, E6).

Against metadata-warehouse v1.10.0 with 0001 to 0003 applied; applied with `patch -p1` in the image build
(modama search stack, warehouse-airflow.Dockerfile; change embedding-fp32-model, D3; @weber, 283).

